Slide 3: The Customization Hierarchy
This slide provides the "Decision Guide" for developers to choose the right tool for the job. The elaboration focuses on the trigger and scope of each customization layer:
Behavioral Layer (Instructions): These are "always-on" and automatically added to every chat request. They are best for global standards, such as "always use four-space indentation" or "apply specific naming conventions to IaC resources"
.
Invocation Layer (Prompt Files): These act as manual, named slash commands (e.g., /security-review). Use these for one-off tasks where you need a repeatable "play" to run against a specific file or code selection
.
Persona Layer (Custom Agents): Unlike instructions, these are selected by the user (or @mentioned). They define a persistent role and, crucially, limit tool access (e.g., a "Planning" agent that can read code but never edit it)
.
Packaging Layer (Skills): These are reusable "runbooks" that include not just instructions, but bundled scripts and templates. They are auto-discovered by Copilot when relevant to the task
.
Connectivity Layer (MCP): The Model Context Protocol is the "missing piece" for external data. It allows agents to pull live context from external systems like GitHub issues, databases, or cloud metadata
.
Policy Layer (Hooks): If instructions are "advice," hooks are hard enforcement. They can approve or deny tool executions (like blocking a command that targets a production database) before they happen
.

