Slide 14: Security, Guardrails, and Specialized Personas
This slide covers the "Safety-First" approach to building agents, specifically for roles like a Security Reviewer or a Domain Expert.
The Principle of Least Privilege: One of the most critical security practices is restricting which tools an agent can use. For instance, a Security Reviewer agent should be configured in the YAML frontmatter with read-only tools (like codebase) while explicitly excluding "destructive" tools like terminal or edit
.
Defining Strict Boundaries: Effective agents use "Always/Never" rules to prevent hallucinations or risky behavior
. Examples include:
"Never modify production configuration files."
"Always check for unused variables before finalizing a refactor"
.
"Ask for user confirmation before executing any npm command"
.
Persona Specificity: To make these agents "meaningful," they must avoid being vague. Instead of a general "Frontend Dev," define a specialist like "Expert in React 18+ focused on performance"
. This ensures the agent uses the latest hooks (like useMemo) rather than outdated patterns it might have in its general training data
.
Organization-Level Deployment: For team collaboration, these agents can be shared at the GitHub Organization level. This allows the entire company to access the same verified "Security Specialist" or "Cloud Architect" agent without every developer needing to copy files manually
.
