Speaker Notes for Slide 14
"The key takeaway for this slide is that these features are not overlapping—they are composable
.
Think of it as a hierarchy of control
. You start with Instructions for always-on guidance, like ensuring your team always uses specific naming conventions
. On top of that, you build Custom Agents to act as your specialists, like a 'Cost Optimizer' that has the persona of an auditor but is restricted from making actual code changes
.
When those agents need to actually do something complex, like running a diagnostic script or generating a post-mortem, they invoke Skills—which are portable runbooks that include scripts and templates
. To make sure your agents aren't hallucinating data, you plug in MCP, allowing them to query live GitHub issues or cloudSkus in real-time
.
Finally, you protect the entire stack with Hooks. If an instruction is 'advice,' a hook is 'enforcement'
. A preToolUse hook can deterministically block an agent from deleting a production database, even if the AI thinks it's a good idea
.
Pro-tip for the team: Always use the smallest primitive that solves the problem
. Don't build a complex agent if a simple instruction file will do the job
."
